## The three problems hooks solved

**1. Reusing stateful logic was painful.** HOCs and render props both worked by *wrapping*, so the tree filled with layers:

```jsx
withRouter(withTheme(connect(mapState)(withAuth(Dashboard))))
```

A custom hook adds nothing to the tree:

```jsx
function Dashboard() {
  const user = useAuth();
  const theme = useTheme();
}
```

**2. Related code was split by lifecycle.** A subscription had to be set up in `componentDidMount`, updated in `componentDidUpdate` and torn down in `componentWillUnmount` — three methods, one concern. Meanwhile each method mixed several unrelated concerns.

```jsx
// One concern, one block, setup and teardown adjacent
useEffect(() => {
  const sub = source.subscribe(handler);
  return () => sub.unsubscribe();
}, [source]);
```

**3. Classes were a barrier.** `this` binding, constructors, and the fact that classes minify poorly and are harder for compilers to optimise.

---

## The built-in hooks

| Hook | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `useState` | Local state |
| `useReducer` | Local state with structured transitions |
| `useEffect` | Synchronise with an external system |
| `useLayoutEffect` | Same, but before the browser paints |
| `useContext` | Read a context value |
| `useRef` | A mutable box that survives renders |
| `useMemo` | Cache a computed value |
| `useCallback` | Cache a function identity |
| `useTransition` | Mark an update as low priority |
| `useDeferredValue` | Render a value at low priority |
| `useId` | Stable SSR-safe unique id |
| `useSyncExternalStore` | Subscribe to an external store safely |

---

## A custom hook is just a function

There is no registration and no special API. Any function whose name starts with `use` and that calls other hooks is a custom hook:

```jsx
function useOnlineStatus() {
  const [online, setOnline] = useState(navigator.onLine);

  useEffect(() => {
    const on = () => setOnline(true);
    const off = () => setOnline(false);
    window.addEventListener('online', on);
    window.addEventListener('offline', off);
    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener('online', on);
      window.removeEventListener('offline', off);
    };
  }, []);

  return online;
}
```

> Each component calling a custom hook gets **its own independent state**. Hooks share logic, never data.

## The two rules

1. **Only call hooks at the top level.** Not in conditions, loops, or nested functions.
2. **Only call hooks from React function components or custom hooks.**

---

## Why — React counts, it does not read names

Internally, hook state is roughly a list per component:

```jsx
function Profile() {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');    // slot 0
  const [age, setAge] = useState(0);       // slot 1
  useEffect(() => { … });                  // slot 2
}
```

On every render React walks the list in order. It has no idea these are called `name` and `age` — it only knows "the first `useState` call", "the second".

```jsx
// ❌ Now the slots shift depending on a runtime value
function Profile({ showAge }) {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');       // slot 0
  if (showAge) {
    const [age, setAge] = useState(0);        // slot 1 — sometimes
  }
  useEffect(() => { … });                     // slot 1 or 2 — depends!
}
```

When `showAge` flips from true to false, the effect that used to be in slot 2 now reads slot 1, which holds the age state. React throws:

> *Rendered fewer hooks than expected.*

---

## The fixes

```jsx
// ✅ Call unconditionally, use the result conditionally
function Profile({ showAge }) {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');
  const [age, setAge] = useState(0);
  return <>{showAge && <p>{age}</p>}</>;
}

// ✅ Or split into two components so each has a fixed hook list
function Profile({ showAge }) {
  return showAge ? <ProfileWithAge /> : <ProfileBasic />;
}
```

---

## Early returns are the sneaky version

```jsx
// ❌ Hooks after a conditional return are conditional
function Profile({ user }) {
  if (!user) return <Spinner />;   // ← early exit
  const [name, setName] = useState(user.name);   // sometimes not called
}

// ✅ All hooks first, then the guard
function Profile({ user }) {
  const [name, setName] = useState(user?.name ?? '');
  if (!user) return <Spinner />;
}
```

---

## Let the linter enforce it

```json
{ "plugins": ["react-hooks"],
  "rules": { "react-hooks/rules-of-hooks": "error" } }
```

This rule is `error`, not `warn`, for a reason — a violation is not a style issue, it is a guaranteed bug the moment the condition changes.

> The `use()` API in React 19 is a deliberate exception: it *can* be called conditionally. It is not spelled `useSomething` precisely to signal that it plays by different rules.

## Use 1 — reaching a DOM node

```jsx
function SearchBox() {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    inputRef.current.focus();   // imperative escape hatch
  }, []);

  return <input ref={inputRef} />;
}
```

The legitimate cases are focus, text selection, media playback, scrolling, measuring, and integrating a non-React library that wants a DOM node.

---

## Use 2 — a value that persists without rendering

```jsx
function Stopwatch() {
  const [elapsed, setElapsed] = useState(0);
  const intervalRef = useRef(null);        // not rendered → not state

  function start() {
    intervalRef.current = setInterval(() => setElapsed((e) => e + 1), 1000);
  }

  function stop() {
    clearInterval(intervalRef.current);
  }

  useEffect(() => () => clearInterval(intervalRef.current), []);

  return <p>{elapsed}s</p>;
}
```

The interval id must survive re-renders, but changing it should not cause one. That is exactly a ref.

---

## Ref vs state

| | `useRef` | `useState` |
|---|---------|-----------|
| Survives re-renders | Yes | Yes |
| Triggers re-render | **No** | Yes |
| Update timing | Immediate, synchronous | Next render |
| Read during render | ❌ Avoid | ✅ Yes |
| Use for | Timer ids, DOM nodes, previous values | Anything displayed |

---

## The rule that keeps you out of trouble

> **Do not read or write `ref.current` during render.**

```jsx
// ❌ Impure — the output depends on something React cannot track
function Bad() {
  const ref = useRef(0);
  ref.current++;
  return <p>{ref.current}</p>;   // won't update reliably; breaks under concurrency
}
```

Refs are for event handlers and effects. Rendering must stay pure.

---

## Tracking the previous value

```jsx
function usePrevious(value) {
  const ref = useRef(undefined);
  useEffect(() => { ref.current = value; });   // after render
  return ref.current;                          // last render's value
}
```

---

## Callback refs

When you need to run code the moment a node attaches or detaches — including for dynamic lists — pass a function instead of a ref object:

```jsx
<div ref={(node) => {
  if (node) observer.observe(node);
  return () => observer.unobserve(node);   // React 19 supports a cleanup return
}} />
```

## The basic shape

```jsx
const ThemeContext = createContext('light');

function App() {
  return (
    <ThemeContext.Provider value="dark">
      <Toolbar />
    </ThemeContext.Provider>
  );
}

function Button() {
  const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);   // reads the nearest provider above
  return <button className={theme} />;
}
```

---

## Limitation 1 — no selectors

```jsx
const value = { user, theme, notifications };

// A component that only reads `theme` still re-renders when a
// notification arrives, because the whole value object changed.
```

Redux, Zustand and Jotai all support subscribing to a slice. Context has no equivalent — the subscription is to the whole value.

**Workaround: split contexts by change frequency.**

```jsx
<UserContext.Provider value={user}>              {/* changes rarely */}
  <ThemeContext.Provider value={theme}>          {/* changes rarely */}
    <NotificationsContext.Provider value={notes}> {/* changes often */}
      <App />
```

---

## Limitation 2 — unstable value identity

```jsx
// ❌ New object every render → every consumer re-renders every time
<AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, login, logout }}>

// ✅ Stable unless the parts actually change
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, login, logout }), [user, login, logout]);
<AuthContext.Provider value={value}>
```

This is the most common context performance bug by a wide margin.

---

## The state/dispatch split

A cheap and effective pattern: put the value and the updater in separate contexts. Components that only dispatch never re-render on state changes.

```jsx
const StateContext = createContext(null);
const DispatchContext = createContext(null);   // dispatch is always stable

<StateContext.Provider value={state}>
  <DispatchContext.Provider value={dispatch}>
    {children}
```

---

## Always wrap it in a custom hook

```jsx
export function useAuth() {
  const ctx = useContext(AuthContext);
  if (!ctx) throw new Error('useAuth must be used within <AuthProvider>');
  return ctx;
}
```

Without the guard, using it outside a provider silently gives you the default value and fails somewhere confusing later.

---

## Context is not a state manager

It is a **dependency-injection mechanism** — it solves "how does this value reach a deep component", not "how is this value managed". Combine it with `useState` or `useReducer` for small cases, and use a real store when updates are frequent or the state is large.

## A complete example

```jsx
function useLocalStorage(key, initialValue) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState(() => {
    try {
      const stored = window.localStorage.getItem(key);
      return stored ? JSON.parse(stored) : initialValue;
    } catch {
      return initialValue;   // private mode, corrupt JSON
    }
  });

  useEffect(() => {
    try {
      window.localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(value));
    } catch {
      // quota exceeded — fail silently rather than crash the app
    }
  }, [key, value]);

  return [value, setValue];   // mirrors useState's API
}

// Usage
const [theme, setTheme] = useLocalStorage('theme', 'light');
```

---

## The rule people misunderstand

> Custom hooks share **logic**, not **state**.

```jsx
function A() { const [c, setC] = useCounter(); }   // A's own counter
function B() { const [c, setC] = useCounter(); }   // B's own, unrelated counter
```

If you want shared *data*, you need Context or a store. This is a very common interview follow-up.

---

## Return-shape convention

| Returns | Shape | Why |
|---------|-------|-----|
| One value | The value | `const online = useOnlineStatus()` |
| Two values | A tuple | Caller can rename freely: `const [x, setX] = …` |
| Three or more | An object | Names stay meaningful, caller can pick |

```jsx
// Tuple — order-dependent, fine for two
return [value, setValue];

// Object — self-documenting, and destructuring is optional
return { data, error, isLoading, refetch };
```

---

## What makes one good

**One responsibility.** `useUserAndCartAndTheme` should be three hooks.

**Cleans up after itself.** Every subscription, timer and listener gets torn down.

**Stable identities.** Wrap returned functions in `useCallback` if consumers may put them in dependency arrays.

**Handles its own errors.** A hook that throws on malformed `localStorage` will crash every app that uses it.

**Named for what it does, not how.** `useOnlineStatus` beats `useNavigatorOnlineEventListener`.

---

## When not to extract one

If the "hook" is a wrapper around a single `useState` with no added behaviour, it is indirection without benefit:

```jsx
// ❌ Adds a file and a name for nothing
function useName() { return useState(''); }
```

Extract when the logic is genuinely reused, or when pulling it out makes the component substantially easier to read.

## Timing

```text
Render → DOM updated → useLayoutEffect → browser paints → useEffect
                       ↑ blocking                        ↑ non-blocking
```

---

## The problem it solves

```jsx
// ❌ With useEffect: the tooltip paints at 0,0, then jumps into place.
// The user sees a flash.
function Tooltip({ targetRef }) {
  const [pos, setPos] = useState({ top: 0, left: 0 });

  useEffect(() => {
    const rect = targetRef.current.getBoundingClientRect();
    setPos({ top: rect.bottom + 8, left: rect.left });
  }, [targetRef]);

  return <div style={pos} className="tooltip" />;
}

// ✅ With useLayoutEffect the measurement and reposition happen
// before paint — the user only ever sees the final position.
useLayoutEffect(() => {
  const rect = targetRef.current.getBoundingClientRect();
  setPos({ top: rect.bottom + 8, left: rect.left });
}, [targetRef]);
```

---

## Comparison

| | `useEffect` | `useLayoutEffect` |
|---|-------------|-------------------|
| Runs | After paint | Before paint |
| Blocks painting | No | **Yes** |
| Use for | Fetching, subscriptions, logging, most things | Measuring and adjusting layout |
| SSR | Skipped, no warning | **Warns** — no DOM on the server |

---

## The SSR warning

> *useLayoutEffect does nothing on the server.*

There is no DOM to measure during server rendering. The usual fix in a shared library:

```jsx
const useIsomorphicLayoutEffect =
  typeof window !== 'undefined' ? useLayoutEffect : useEffect;
```

In application code it is normally better to make the component client-only than to paper over it.

---

## Why the default should stay `useEffect`

`useLayoutEffect` is synchronous and blocks the browser. Slow work inside it delays paint directly, which shows up as jank on low-end devices. Fetching data there gains nothing and delays first paint for the whole page.

> The heuristic: **if the user could see the intermediate state and it would look wrong, use `useLayoutEffect`. Otherwise use `useEffect`.**

That is a short list — tooltips, popovers, auto-resizing textareas, scroll restoration, measuring before an animation.

## The problem

Typing in a search box that filters 10,000 rows. Each keystroke triggers an expensive render, React blocks, and the input visibly lags.

---

## useTransition — when you own the update

```jsx
function Search() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [results, setResults] = useState([]);
  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();

  function handleChange(e) {
    setQuery(e.target.value);          // urgent — must feel instant

    startTransition(() => {
      setResults(filterHugeList(e.target.value));   // interruptible
    });
  }

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={handleChange} />
      <div style={{ opacity: isPending ? 0.5 : 1 }}>
        <Results items={results} />
      </div>
    </>
  );
}
```

If another keystroke arrives mid-render, React **abandons** the in-progress render and starts over with the new input. The text field never stutters.

---

## useDeferredValue — when you receive the value

```jsx
function Search({ query }) {         // query is a prop — you cannot wrap its setter
  const deferredQuery = useDeferredValue(query);
  const isStale = query !== deferredQuery;

  return (
    <div style={{ opacity: isStale ? 0.5 : 1 }}>
      <Results query={deferredQuery} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

React renders with the old value first, then re-renders with the new one at low priority.

---

## Choosing

| | `useTransition` | `useDeferredValue` |
|---|-----------------|--------------------|
| You control the setter | ✅ | Not required |
| Wraps | The update function | A value |
| Gives you | `isPending` | Compare old vs new yourself |
| Typical use | Tab switch, route change, filter | Value arriving as a prop |

---

## What they do not fix

They make rendering interruptible. They do **not**:

- Reduce network requests → that is debouncing
- Make the slow render faster → that is memoisation or virtualisation
- Help if the bottleneck is one long synchronous function → React can only interrupt *between* components

> If a single component takes 300ms, a transition cannot pause inside it. Fix the component first.

---

## Also worth knowing

Wrapping the child in `memo` is often required for these to help at all. If the expensive subtree re-renders regardless, deferring the value changes nothing.

## The problem

```jsx
// ❌ Server generates one id, client generates a different one → hydration mismatch
function Field({ label }) {
  const id = `input-${Math.random()}`;
  return (
    <>
      <label htmlFor={id}>{label}</label>
      <input id={id} />
    </>
  );
}
```

A hardcoded id is worse — render the component twice and you have duplicate ids, which breaks the label association entirely.

---

## The fix

```jsx
import { useId } from 'react';

function Field({ label, hint }) {
  const id = useId();

  return (
    <>
      <label htmlFor={id}>{label}</label>
      <input id={id} aria-describedby={`${id}-hint`} />
      <p id={`${id}-hint`}>{hint}</p>
    </>
  );
}
```

React derives the id from the component's position in the tree, so the server and client produce identical output.

---

## One call, many ids

Do not call `useId` per element — derive suffixes from one call:

```jsx
function Signup() {
  const id = useId();
  return (
    <>
      <label htmlFor={`${id}-email`}>Email</label>
      <input id={`${id}-email`} />

      <label htmlFor={`${id}-password`}>Password</label>
      <input id={`${id}-password`} />
    </>
  );
}
```

---

## What it is not for

```jsx
// ❌ Wrong on two counts: hooks cannot be called in a loop,
// and a key must be derived from the data
{items.map((item) => <Row key={useId()} />)}
```

A key identifies *which item this is* across renders. A generated id changes nothing about identity — it just happens to be unique. Use `item.id`.

---

## Where the ids come from

They look like `:r0:` or `«r1»` — deliberately containing characters that are invalid in CSS selectors, so nobody is tempted to write `#:r0:` in a stylesheet. Use a ref if you need to select the element.

---

## Why this matters beyond React

The `label`/`input` pairing is what lets a screen reader announce the field, and what makes clicking the label focus the input. Getting ids wrong in a reusable component silently breaks accessibility everywhere it is used — which is exactly why React added a hook for it.

## The shape

```jsx
function FancyInput({ ref, ...props }) {   // React 19: ref is a normal prop
  const inputRef = useRef(null);

  useImperativeHandle(ref, () => ({
    focus() { inputRef.current.focus(); },
    clear() { inputRef.current.value = ''; },
    // The raw node is deliberately NOT exposed
  }), []);

  return <input ref={inputRef} {...props} />;
}

// Parent
function Form() {
  const fancyRef = useRef(null);
  return (
    <>
      <FancyInput ref={fancyRef} />
      <button onClick={() => fancyRef.current.focus()}>Focus</button>
    </>
  );
}
```

Before React 19 this required wrapping the component in `forwardRef`. In React 19 `ref` is passed as an ordinary prop and `forwardRef` is no longer needed.

---

## Why restrict the surface

Without it, the parent gets the DOM node and can do anything — change styles, read `innerHTML`, reparent it. Your component can no longer guarantee its own invariants. Exposing two named methods keeps the contract explicit.

---

## Justified vs not

| Justified | Not justified |
|-----------|---------------|
| `focus()` | `setValue()` → make it a controlled prop |
| `scrollIntoView()` | `setDisabled()` → prop |
| `play()` / `pause()` on media | `getValue()` → lift state up |
| `open()` / `close()` on a dialog | `rerender()` → you have a state bug |
| Resetting a wrapped third-party widget | Anything derivable from props |

> Every imperative method you add is a piece of state living outside React. The declarative version is almost always simpler to reason about — reach for this only when there is no declarative equivalent.

---

## Dependencies matter

```jsx
useImperativeHandle(ref, () => ({
  submit() { onSubmit(value); },
}), [onSubmit, value]);   // ← without these, submit closes over stale values
```

Same stale-closure risk as any other hook with a dependency array.

---

## The realistic use case

Wrapping an imperative library — a video player, a map, a rich-text editor — where the underlying API is genuinely method-based. You wrap it once and expose a narrow, documented set of methods, rather than letting every consumer reach into the library instance.

## Tearing, the problem it solves

Under concurrent rendering React can pause mid-render. If an external store changes during that pause, components rendered before the pause show the old value and components after show the new one — in the same committed frame. That inconsistency is called **tearing**.

React state cannot tear, because React controls when it changes. External stores can.

---

## The API

```jsx
const value = useSyncExternalStore(
  subscribe,        // (callback) => unsubscribe
  getSnapshot,      // () => current value, must be referentially stable
  getServerSnapshot // optional, for SSR
);
```

---

## A real example

```jsx
function subscribe(callback) {
  window.addEventListener('online', callback);
  window.addEventListener('offline', callback);
  return () => {
    window.removeEventListener('online', callback);
    window.removeEventListener('offline', callback);
  };
}

function useOnlineStatus() {
  return useSyncExternalStore(
    subscribe,
    () => navigator.onLine,   // client snapshot
    () => true                // server snapshot — assume online
  );
}
```

Compare that with the `useState` + `useEffect` version: it can miss an event fired between render and effect, and it has no answer for SSR.

---

## The critical rule

**`getSnapshot` must return a cached value, not a new object each call.**

```jsx
// ❌ New array every call → React re-renders forever
const items = useSyncExternalStore(subscribe, () => store.items.filter(Boolean));

// ✅ Return the stored reference; do the derivation with useMemo afterwards
const items = useSyncExternalStore(subscribe, () => store.items);
const visible = useMemo(() => items.filter(Boolean), [items]);
```

React calls `getSnapshot` and compares with `Object.is`. A fresh object always compares unequal, so it re-renders, calls it again, and loops.

---

## Who actually calls this

Mostly **library authors**. Redux, Zustand, Jotai and Valtio all use it internally so their stores work correctly with concurrent features. In application code you would use it for browser APIs that are not already wrapped — media queries, online status, geolocation, `document.visibilityState`.

> Knowing *why* it exists — tearing under concurrent rendering — is what the question is really testing.
